# =============================================================================
# CityFlow AI — environment variables
# =============================================================================
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE
#   1. Make a copy of this file in the same folder and name the copy ".env"
#   2. Replace the placeholder values below with your real values
#   3. NEVER commit ".env" to GitHub (it is already in .gitignore)
#
# Only the first two are required. Everything else has a working default, and
# the product degrades honestly when a service is not configured rather than
# breaking.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. DATABASE — REQUIRED (Neon, or any Postgres)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Paste the "Pooled connection" string from your Neon dashboard.
# It looks like:
#   postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxxx-pooler.region.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. AUTH SECRET — REQUIRED (signs login cookies)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Must be a long random string, at least 32 characters.
# Generate one on your Mac or Linux terminal with:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
AUTH_SECRET="replace-with-a-long-random-string-at-least-32-characters"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. PUBLIC APP URL
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Used in the links inside verification and password-reset emails, so it must
# match how people actually reach the site.
#   Local development: http://localhost:3000
#   Docker Compose:    http://localhost:8080
#   Production:        https://your-project-name.vercel.app
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. ML SERVICE — optional
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Python service that runs Prophet, XGBoost and the OR-Tools optimiser.
#
# LEAVE THIS BLANK and the web application uses its own built-in TypeScript
# demand model instead. That is a supported configuration, not a broken one:
# recommendations still work, forecasts are a seasonal prior rather than a
# fitted model, and the Admin Portal says which is in use.
#
#   Docker Compose:  http://ml:8000
#   Local Python:    http://localhost:8000
#   Hosted:          https://your-ml-service.onrender.com
ML_SERVICE_URL=""

# Shared secret. Must match CITYFLOW_API_KEY in the ML service's own .env.
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
ML_SERVICE_KEY=""

# How long to wait before falling back to the built-in model, in milliseconds.
# Kept short on purpose: a free-tier container that has gone to sleep can take
# 30 seconds to wake, and nobody waits 30 seconds for a departure time.
ML_SERVICE_TIMEOUT_MS="2500"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. EMAIL — optional
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Powers email confirmation and password reset.
#
# LEAVE THIS BLANK and those emails are printed to the server terminal instead,
# with the link in full. That is genuinely usable in development — you click the
# link out of your terminal and the flow works end to end. The link is NEVER
# returned in an HTTP response, because that would let anybody request a reset
# for a stranger's address and read the link out of the reply.
#
# Get a free key at resend.com. Without a verified domain you can only send to
# your own address, which is enough to test with.
RESEND_API_KEY=""

# Must be a domain verified with your provider. Resend's sandbox sender works
# for testing without any domain setup.
EMAIL_FROM="CityFlow AI <onboarding@resend.dev>"
